    "prompt_tokens": 532,
    "raw": "Given:\n- Jeff takes 6 cans from Tim.\n- Tim buys another half the amount of soda cans he had left.\n- Tim ends up with 24 cans.\n\nUnknown:\n- How many cans of soda did Tim have at first?\n\nOperations:\n1. Subtract the number of cans Jeff took from the initial amount.\n2. Add half of the remaining cans to the result from step 1.\n\nConcrete check:\nLet \\( x \\) be the initial number of cans Tim had.\n\n1. After Jeff takes 6 cans, Tim has \\( x - 6 \\) cans.\n2. Tim buys another half of what he has left, which is \\( \\frac{1}{2}(x - 6) \\).\n3. Adding this to what he had left, Tim now has \\( (x - 6) + \\frac{1}{2}(x - 6) = \\frac{3}{2}(x - 6) \\) cans.\n4. We know this equals 24 cans, so we set up the equation:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3}{2}(x - 6) = 24\n   \\]\n5. Solving for \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   x - 6 = \\frac{24 \\times 2}{3} = 16\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = 16 + 6 = 22\n   \\]\n\nSo, Tim initially had 22 cans of soda.\n\nCHECK: \n- Jeff takes 6 cans, leaving Tim with 16 cans.\n- Tim buys another half of 16 cans, which is 8 cans.\n- Adding these, Tim has \\( 16 + 8 = 24 \\) cans, which matches the problem statement.\n\nFINAL: 22",
